<http://data.silknow.org/object/a0d838a0-1a1e-3a87-b84b-4ad03a572933>
        a                   ecrm:E73_Information_Object ;
        rdfs:comment        "June 10, 2014 - March 8, 2015" , "With the establishment of its colonial trade center in Goa, India, in the early 16th century, Portugal was flooded with imported Indian luxury textiles, which left a distinct mark on furnishings such as this marriage quilt, or colcha. Abounding with painstakingly embroidered carnations and scrolling vines on a silk ground, it was either made in India for the Portuguese market or crafted in Portugal as a version of the Indian originals. The artisans who made this piece substituted the carnation—a flower indigenous to the Mediterranean—for a lotus-blossom motif that would have been more familiar to the Asian market." , "“Insnared with flowers, I fall on grass\"" ;
        ecrm:P129_is_about  <http://data.silknow.org/object/27f18f43-21ce-3082-ac87-ef92a2cd4f83> ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    "Exhibition" ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "With the establishment of its colonial trade center in Goa, India, in the early 16th century, Portugal was flooded with imported Indian luxury textiles, which left a distinct mark on furnishings such as this marriage quilt, or colcha. Abounding with painstakingly embroidered carnations and scrolling vines on a silk ground, it was either made in India for the Portuguese market or crafted in Portugal as a version of the Indian originals. The artisans who made this piece substituted the carnation—a flower indigenous to the Mediterranean—for a lotus-blossom motif that would have been more familiar to the Asian market." , "“Insnared with flowers, I fall on grass\"" , "June 10, 2014 - March 8, 2015" ;
        schema:contentUrl   <https://risdmuseum.org/exhibitions-events/exhibitions/insnared-flowers-i-fall-grass> .
